\title{Fast Functions for Prime Numbers}
\description{
Utility functions for dealing with prime numbers, including primality
testing, prime generation, prime factorization, k-tuples, GCD/LCM, and
Euler's totient function. Most functions are vectorized and all core
computations are implemented in C++ via \pkg{Rcpp}.
}
\section{Performance}{

Primes are generated with an optimized Sieve of Eratosthenes, and greatest
common divisors are found with Euclid's algorithm. Inputs that are already
integers (e.g., \code{5L} or \code{1:10}) skip the checks described under \emph{Input
handling}, so they are the fastest to process.
}

\section{Input handling}{

The functions in this package work on 32-bit integers. R's default numeric
type is \code{double}, so a call like \code{is_prime(7)} is fine; the value is checked
and converted to an integer before it is used. A \code{double} input is handled
as follows:
\itemize{
\item Values outside the 32-bit integer range
(\eqn{\pm 2{,}147{,}483{,}647}) raise an error, rather than silently
becoming \code{NA}.
\item Infinite values (\code{Inf}, \code{-Inf}) raise an error.
\item Values that are not whole numbers (e.g., \code{5.3}) are truncated
toward zero (to \code{5}) with a warning.
}

Only numbers are accepted. \code{logical} input, including a bare \code{NA}, is an
error; use \code{NA_integer_} to pass a missing value.
}

\section{NA handling}{

Missing values in a vector are propagated element-wise. For example,
\code{is_prime(c(7L, NA, 9L))} returns \verb{TRUE NA FALSE}, and \code{gcd(c(12L, NA), 8L)}
returns \verb{4 NA}. This applies to \code{\link{is_prime}},
\code{\link{next_prime}}, \code{\link{prev_prime}}, \code{\link{gcd}},
\code{\link{scm}}, \code{\link{coprime}}, and similar vectorized functions.

A missing value is an error where the function needs an actual value to
work with: scalar arguments such as \code{n} in \code{\link{generate_n_primes}}
or \code{min} and \code{max} in \code{\link{generate_primes}}, the \code{tuple} argument of
\code{\link{k_tuple}}, and the \code{upper_bound} argument of
\code{\link{prime_count}} (which must be \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}).
}

\section{Negative numbers}{

Some functions have a sensible answer for zero and negative numbers:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link{is_prime}}: returns \code{NA}, because primality is only
defined for natural numbers. Note that this is not the same as \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{\link{next_prime}} / \code{\link{prev_prime}}: negative values
are valid starting points for the search.
\item \code{\link{prime_factors}}: returns \code{integer(0)} for values less
than 2.
\item \code{\link{gcd}}, \code{\link{scm}}, \code{\link{coprime}}: the sign
of the inputs is ignored, so \code{gcd(-12L, 18L)} is \code{6}.
}

Other functions are not defined for zero or negative numbers and raise an
error:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link{phi}}: Euler's totient is defined for positive integers.
\item \code{\link{prime_count}} / \code{\link{nth_prime_estimate}}: the
estimates are built on the natural logarithm, \eqn{\log(n)}, which needs
a positive \eqn{n}.
}
}

\section{Disabling validation}{

The checks on \code{double} input can be turned off for the whole R session with
\code{options(primes.validate_inputs = FALSE)}. The values are then converted
with \code{as.integer()} and nothing else is checked, so out-of-range values
become \code{NA} and non-whole numbers are truncated without a warning. This is
not recommended for interactive work, but it can save time in a
long-running analysis whose inputs are already known to be valid. Checks for
\code{NA} and for positivity are always applied, regardless of this setting.
}

